{
  "version": 1,
  "courseId": 756150,
  "courseSlug": "the-complete-guide-to-angular-2",
  "courseTitle": "Angular - The Complete Guide",
  "sourceLanguage": "en",
  "targetLanguage": "zh-TW",
  "lectures": [
    {
      "id": "6656534",
      "title": "Module Introduction",
      "lectureOrder": 494,
      "videoOrder": 454,
      "sourceHash": "95ba520cef2f37a58ddb1cb182d1449bfa8109afb7ba1da25778683cb8f0258d",
      "cues": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "start": 2.7,
          "end": 4.68,
          "en": "-: Welcome to this course module.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "start": 4.68,
          "end": 7.38,
          "en": "In the last course module, about routing,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "start": 7.38,
          "end": 10.8,
          "en": "we had first contact with observables.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "start": 10.8,
          "end": 15.03,
          "en": "Now, I want to dive deeper into observables in this section.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 5,
          "start": 15.03,
          "end": 17.52,
          "en": "Now, you could create a whole course about observables.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 6,
          "start": 17.52,
          "end": 20.37,
          "en": "So we're not going to dive that deeply into them,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 7,
          "start": 20.37,
          "end": 23.61,
          "en": "but I want you to understand what observables actually are",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 8,
          "start": 23.61,
          "end": 27.33,
          "en": "and what you could use them for and why Angular uses them.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 9,
          "start": 27.33,
          "end": 29.28,
          "en": "So what is an observable?",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 10,
          "start": 29.28,
          "end": 33.33,
          "en": "An observable basically can be thought of as a data source.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 11,
          "start": 33.33,
          "end": 35.19,
          "en": "Now, in our Angular project,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 12,
          "start": 35.19,
          "end": 37.23,
          "en": "an observable, basically, just is",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 13,
          "start": 37.23,
          "end": 42.23,
          "en": "an object we import from a third party package, RxJS.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 14,
          "start": 42.81,
          "end": 46.11,
          "en": "The observable here is implemented in a way",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 15,
          "start": 46.11,
          "end": 48.93,
          "en": "that it follows the observable pattern.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 16,
          "start": 48.93,
          "end": 52.02,
          "en": "So we have an observable and we have an observer.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 17,
          "start": 52.02,
          "end": 55.08,
          "en": "In between, we have a stream, a timeline",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 18,
          "start": 55.08,
          "end": 59.43,
          "en": "and on this timeline we can have multiple events emitted",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 19,
          "start": 59.43,
          "end": 62.67,
          "en": "by the observable or data packages, you could say,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 20,
          "start": 62.67,
          "end": 64.32,
          "en": "emitted by that observable,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 21,
          "start": 64.32,
          "end": 68.19,
          "en": "depending on the data source of that observable, of course.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 22,
          "start": 68.19,
          "end": 71.4,
          "en": "So the observable could emit data",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 23,
          "start": 71.4,
          "end": 73.28999999999999,
          "en": "because you trigger it to do so.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 24,
          "start": 73.28999999999999,
          "end": 75.3,
          "en": "You can do that programmatically.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 25,
          "start": 75.3,
          "end": 77.07,
          "en": "It could be connected to a button.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 26,
          "start": 77.07,
          "end": 78.81,
          "en": "And therefore, whenever the button is clicked,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 27,
          "start": 78.81,
          "end": 82.26,
          "en": "an event in a data package is emitted automatically,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 28,
          "start": 82.26,
          "end": 85.35,
          "en": "or as the Angular HTTP service does it,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 29,
          "start": 85.35,
          "end": 87.63,
          "en": "it's connected to HTTP request.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 30,
          "start": 87.63,
          "end": 89.25,
          "en": "So when the response returns,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 31,
          "start": 89.25,
          "end": 91.86,
          "en": "the response is emitted as a data package.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 32,
          "start": 91.86,
          "end": 94.89,
          "en": "And there are dozens of other data sources too.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 33,
          "start": 94.89,
          "end": 99.15,
          "en": "We will have a look on where to find more soon.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 34,
          "start": 99.15,
          "end": 102.50999999999999,
          "en": "So, as I said, the other part is the observer.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 35,
          "start": 102.50999999999999,
          "end": 105.15,
          "en": "This actually is your code, you could say.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 36,
          "start": 105.15,
          "end": 108.03,
          "en": "It's the subscribe function you saw earlier",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 37,
          "start": 108.03,
          "end": 110.75999999999999,
          "en": "or at least it has something to do with that.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 38,
          "start": 110.75999999999999,
          "end": 114.72,
          "en": "There, you have three ways of handling data packages.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 39,
          "start": 114.72,
          "end": 116.52000000000001,
          "en": "You can handle the normal data,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 40,
          "start": 116.52000000000001,
          "end": 118.35,
          "en": "you can handle errors,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 41,
          "start": 118.35,
          "end": 121.14,
          "en": "or you can handle the completion of the observable,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 42,
          "start": 121.14,
          "end": 122.76,
          "en": "because these are the three types",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 43,
          "start": 122.76,
          "end": 124.98,
          "en": "of data packages you can receive.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 44,
          "start": 124.98,
          "end": 128.43,
          "en": "And in this, in these hooks, in these boxes,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 45,
          "start": 128.43,
          "end": 131.28,
          "en": "you could say, your code gets executed.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 46,
          "start": 131.28,
          "end": 133.47,
          "en": "So you can determine what should happen",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 47,
          "start": 133.47,
          "end": 135.27,
          "en": "if I receive a new data package?",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 48,
          "start": 135.27,
          "end": 137.49,
          "en": "What should happen if I receive an error?",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 49,
          "start": 137.49,
          "end": 141.42000000000002,
          "en": "What should happen when the observable eventually completes?",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 50,
          "start": 141.42000000000002,
          "end": 144.69,
          "en": "Side note, an observable doesn't have to complete.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 51,
          "start": 144.69,
          "end": 146.57999999999998,
          "en": "There are observables, for example,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 52,
          "start": 146.57999999999998,
          "end": 149.94,
          "en": "hooked up to a normal button which never complete,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 53,
          "start": 149.94,
          "end": 152.28,
          "en": "because how would you know when it completes?",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 54,
          "start": 152.28,
          "end": 153.66,
          "en": "A user could click the button",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 55,
          "start": 153.66,
          "end": 155.79,
          "en": "how often the user wants, right?",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 56,
          "start": 155.79,
          "end": 158.82,
          "en": "Other observables, like the HTTP request on the other hand,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 57,
          "start": 158.82,
          "end": 162.09,
          "en": "will have a clear end and will complete eventually,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 58,
          "start": 162.09,
          "end": 165.06,
          "en": "because once the response is there, what else should happen?",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 59,
          "start": 165.06,
          "end": 166.68,
          "en": "It's done.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 60,
          "start": 166.68,
          "end": 170.52,
          "en": "So this is how the observable pattern generally works.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 61,
          "start": 170.52,
          "end": 174.6,
          "en": "And of course, you use it to handle asynchronous tasks,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 62,
          "start": 174.6,
          "end": 176.94,
          "en": "because all these data sources here,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 63,
          "start": 176.94,
          "end": 181.71,
          "en": "user events, triggered in your code or a HTTP request,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 64,
          "start": 181.71,
          "end": 183.24,
          "en": "are asynchronous tasks.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 65,
          "start": 183.24,
          "end": 184.83,
          "en": "You don't know when they will happen",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 66,
          "start": 184.83,
          "end": 187.38,
          "en": "and you don't know how long they will take.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 67,
          "start": 187.38,
          "end": 190.98,
          "en": "So if you execute your normal application code,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 68,
          "start": 190.98,
          "end": 193.56,
          "en": "you don't want to wait for these events",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 69,
          "start": 193.56,
          "end": 194.73,
          "en": "or you don't want to",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 70,
          "start": 194.73,
          "end": 197.7,
          "en": "wait for the completion of such a HTTP request",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 71,
          "start": 197.7,
          "end": 199.74,
          "en": "because that would block your program,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 72,
          "start": 199.74,
          "end": 201.66,
          "en": "would block your logic.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 73,
          "start": 201.66,
          "end": 204.18,
          "en": "Therefore, we'd need methods",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 74,
          "start": 204.18,
          "end": 206.43,
          "en": "of handling such asynchronous tasks.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 75,
          "start": 206.43,
          "end": 210.03,
          "en": "And historically you might have used callbacks or promises",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 76,
          "start": 210.03,
          "end": 213,
          "en": "and it's not necessarily bad to use them.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 77,
          "start": 213,
          "end": 216.54,
          "en": "Observables is just a different approach of handling that,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 78,
          "start": 216.54,
          "end": 218.13,
          "en": "a different alternative.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 79,
          "start": 218.13,
          "end": 220.86,
          "en": "And Angular embraces observables,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 80,
          "start": 220.86,
          "end": 223.41,
          "en": "which is why, well, I chose to explain these,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 81,
          "start": 223.41,
          "end": 225.54,
          "en": "because Angular uses them a lot.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 82,
          "start": 225.54,
          "end": 228.72,
          "en": "And actually observables have one major advantage,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 83,
          "start": 228.72,
          "end": 229.86,
          "en": "their operators,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 84,
          "start": 229.86,
          "end": 232.77,
          "en": "which I will show later in this section too.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 85,
          "start": 232.77,
          "end": 234.15,
          "en": "Back to this slide.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 86,
          "start": 234.15,
          "end": 236.57999999999998,
          "en": "So we got our observable and we got our observer",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 87,
          "start": 236.57999999999998,
          "end": 238.86,
          "en": "with the free buckets, you could say,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 88,
          "start": 238.86,
          "end": 242.46,
          "en": "or with the three hooks where different code is executed,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 89,
          "start": 242.46,
          "end": 245.22,
          "en": "depending on the type of package we receive.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 90,
          "start": 245.22,
          "end": 247.11,
          "en": "And then exactly this happens.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 91,
          "start": 247.11,
          "end": 250.89,
          "en": "The observable might emit a couple of normal data packages,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 92,
          "start": 250.89,
          "end": 252.6,
          "en": "it might emit an error,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 93,
          "start": 252.6,
          "end": 255.3,
          "en": "or eventually it might get completed",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 94,
          "start": 255.3,
          "end": 258.18,
          "en": "and the respective code is then executed.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 95,
          "start": 258.18,
          "end": 259.56,
          "en": "So that's the theory.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 96,
          "start": 259.56,
          "end": 263.64300000000003,
          "en": "Let's now take a closer look in our actual project.",
          "zh": ""
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "errors": []
}
